SEG = "PAR"
CAT = "fnswParts"
Q = [
    ("According to the NSW Police users guide, a rifle takes its name from:",
     "Its rifled barrel", ["Its long stock", "Its bolt action", "Its telescopic sight"],
     "The users guide says a rifle derives its name from its rifled barrel, which has grooves and lands on the inside."),
    ("Rifling makes a projectile spin on its axis. The users guide says this gives:",
     "Better accuracy and range", ["Less recoil and less noise", "Wider shot pattern", "Faster reloading"],
     "The users guide says the rifling causes the projectile to spin, leading to greater accuracy and a longer range."),
    ("According to the NSW Police users guide, a shotgun usually has:",
     "Smoothbore barrel", ["Rifled barrel", "Revolving cylinder", "Rifled slug barrel"],
     "The users guide says shotguns usually have a smoothbore barrel without lands and grooves, firing slugs or pellets, and are usually used for moving targets."),
    ("Under the Firearms Act, a pistol is a firearm that is reasonably capable of being:",
     "Raised and fired by one hand", ["Fired only from a rest", "Fired only from the shoulder", "Carried in a pocket"],
     "Section 4 defines a pistol as a firearm reasonably capable of being raised and fired by one hand that does not exceed any prescribed dimension."),
    ("On a break-open single or double barrel firearm, opening the action:",
     "Shows if it is loaded", ["Sets the safety catch", "Fires the second barrel", "Locks the barrels shut"],
     "The users guide says opening a break-open action reveals whether it is loaded, and the firearm cannot be fired while it is open."),
    ("A break-open single or double barrel firearm generally must be:",
     "Hand-loaded after each shot", ["Fed from a box magazine", "Cocked with a side lever", "Loaded through the muzzle"],
     "The users guide says break-open firearms generally have no mechanical reloading mechanism such as a magazine and must be hand-loaded after each shot."),
    ("On a lever action firearm, the lever is usually located:",
     "By the trigger guard", ["On top of the receiver", "Beside the muzzle", "Inside the fore-end"],
     "The users guide says lever action firearms have a lever or handle located under, near or as part of the trigger guard, and operating it opens the action."),
    ("According to the NSW Police users guide, self-loading firearms can also be called:",
     "Semi-automatic", ["Fully automatic", "Break-action", "Single shot"],
     "The users guide says self-loaders fire one cartridge per pull of the trigger and can also be called semi-automatic firearms."),
    ("In the NSW Police users guide, the receiver is:",
     "Frame for the moving parts", ["Tube the bullet leaves by", "Part set into the shoulder", "Container for cartridges"],
     "The users guide says the receiver is the frame which contains the operating parts, including the breech and firing mechanism."),
    ("The inside of a firearm's barrel is called the:",
     "Bore", ["Breech", "Muzzle", "Throat"],
     "The users guide says the barrel is the tube the projectile passes through, its exit end is the muzzle and its inside is the bore."),
    ("In the NSW Police users guide's list of basic functions, 'lock' means:",
     "The bolt seals the breech", ["The safety catch goes on", "The magazine is removed", "The trigger is released"],
     "The users guide says in the lock function the breech, bolt and breech face come together to form a securely sealed unit that fully supports the cartridge."),
    ("In the users guide's list of basic functions, 'cock' means placing a firearm in a condition where it:",
     "Can be discharged", ["Cannot be fired", "Is fully unloaded", "Is ready to clean"],
     "The users guide defines cocking as placing a firearm into a condition where it can be discharged."),
    ("Where is the primer in rimfire ammunition?",
     "Around the rim's inner edge", ["In the centre of the case head", "Behind the projectile", "Mixed into the powder"],
     "The users guide says the primer in rimfire cartridges is found around the inner edges of the cartridge case, while centrefire primers sit in the middle of the case head."),
    ("Why can many centrefire cartridges be reloaded?",
     "Their primer can be replaced", ["Their brass case is very thin", "Their bullet is reusable", "They need no primer"],
     "The users guide says the centrefire primer is in the middle of the case head and can be replaced, which is why many centrefire cartridges can be reloaded."),
    ("Which type of cartridge cannot be reloaded, according to the NSW Police users guide?",
     "Rimfire", ["Centrefire rifle", "Centrefire pistol", "Shotgun shell"],
     "The users guide says rimfire cartridges often have a thin case, are limited to lower pressure calibres and cannot be reloaded."),
    ("The NSW Police users guide says most modern rimfire cartridges do not exceed:",
     ".22 calibre", [".303 calibre", ".308 calibre", ".38 calibre"],
     "The users guide says most modern rimfire cartridges do not exceed .22 calibre, because the thin rimfire case limits them to lower pressures."),
    ("According to the National Firearms Safety Code, black powder is made from:",
     "Saltpetre, charcoal and sulphur", ["Nitro-cellulose and nitro-glycerine", "Lead, tin and antimony", "Copper and zinc dust"],
     "The Code's glossary describes black powder as a propellant developed centuries ago from saltpetre, charcoal and sulphur."),
    ("According to the Code, the main ingredient of single-base smokeless powder is:",
     "Nitro-cellulose", ["Black powder", "Powdered charcoal", "Nitro-glycerine"],
     "The Code says single-base smokeless powder is mainly nitro-cellulose, while double-base powder adds a percentage of nitro-glycerine."),
    ("In the National Firearms Safety Code's glossary, fouling is:",
     "Residue left in the barrel", ["Cracked firing pin", "Bent cartridge rim", "Loose telescopic sight mount"],
     "The Code defines fouling as a deposit of residue from burning powder or from bullet metal on the interior surface of a barrel."),
    ("In the National Firearms Safety Code's glossary, the throat is:",
     "Where the chamber meets the bore", ["Front end of the muzzle", "Rear face of the bolt", "Base of the primer pocket"],
     "The Code defines the throat as the forward portion of the chamber where it tapers to meet the diameter of the bore proper."),
    ("In the National Firearms Safety Code's glossary, a shotgun's pattern is:",
     "Its spread of pellets", ["Engraving on its action", "Choke on its barrel", "Length of its shells"],
     "The Code defines pattern as the distribution of pellets from a shotgun at a given distance in a given area."),
    ("In the National Firearms Safety Code's glossary, the sear is the part that:",
     "Holds the hammer or pin cocked", ["Throws the empty case clear", "Seals the bore at the muzzle", "Grips the cartridge rim"],
     "The Code defines the sear as a part designed to hold the hammer or firing pin at half or full cock."),
    ("In the National Firearms Safety Code's glossary, the anvil is a component of the:",
     "Primer", ["Bolt", "Trigger", "Stock"],
     "The Code says the anvil is part of the primer, against which the priming composition is crushed by the impact of the firing pin."),
    ("According to the NSW Police users guide, an air rifle propels its projectile by:",
     "Compressed gas or a spring", ["Small smokeless powder charge", "Primer alone", "Electric charge"],
     "The users guide says an air rifle uses compressed air or another gas, not gas from an explosive, or a spring. The Act's definition of firearm includes an air gun."),
]
